WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:04.143
Hey! Welcome to the last video of Project&nbsp;
Kamp, season 3.

00:00:04.143 --> 00:00:09.071
In the previous video you saw us removing this big batch of invasive&nbsp;mimosa trees next to the office.

00:00:09.071 --> 00:00:14.200
They removed to prevent the little house from burning down&nbsp;in case of a fire and as a test we cut the

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:18.920
trees at one meter above the ground. I know it looked&nbsp;
weird but it's done because it makes it easier

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:22.988
to remove the sprouts that grow back, since&nbsp;
you don't have to bend to the floor to take&nbsp;them out.

00:00:23.494 --> 00:00:27.640
We've done this a few times taking out&nbsp;
these sprouts but they continue to grow back.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:31.395
So it's time for the next step: a digger to remove&nbsp;
all the stumps and their roots.

00:00:31.395 --> 00:00:37.310
And after that we can plant new fruit trees because, well, it's&nbsp;tasty and those trees are also much more fire&nbsp;resilient.

00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:41.880
But before that, we want to save the&nbsp;small native oaks that started to grow here.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:48.320
So we are removing them to replant them later on. Here is one oak that has some brambles around it.

00:00:58.242 --> 00:01:00.396
Ok, so we now have this.

00:01:05.181 --> 00:01:08.880
Now one it's done. It's time for doing it the others.

00:01:17.361 --> 00:01:19.145
And then the digger came.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:25.427
It's impressive to see a&nbsp;digger working, everything looks easier.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:35.022
The digger just finished up for today.

00:02:35.022 --> 00:02:40.440
He did all this amazing job. Tomorrow&nbsp;he will continue on the bottom part there.

00:02:51.412 --> 00:02:55.472
- What were you doing Gui?
- I'm removing the&nbsp;small ones so they don't sprout

00:02:55.472 --> 00:03:01.040
since  the shovel is too big for this ones&nbsp;
it's better than do it, to do it by hand.

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:24.726
So as you can see, we have removed all the stumps.

00:04:24.726 --> 00:04:29.885
Now we have them scattered around, we need to push them aside

00:04:29.885 --> 00:04:36.560
and starting to remove&nbsp;the slope, so we don't have a such a steep part.

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:16.175
The stumps of the mimosas are now gone, so&nbsp;
this is what it looks like from above.

00:05:16.175 --> 00:05:20.935
It's quite impressive to see the amount of energy&nbsp;
it takes to remove invasive tree species.

00:05:22.375 --> 00:05:25.037
But now we can go on to the next step.

00:05:25.037 --> 00:05:31.317
So we have&nbsp;here these five buckets. We're going to go to the neighbor George and get some cow manure.

00:05:31.317 --> 00:05:35.404
That's what we need for the trees and the... trees.

00:05:44.314 --> 00:05:49.280
And when we got here it&nbsp;turned out we didn't need the buckets, we just dumped a shitload in our precious pickup.

00:05:52.820 --> 00:05:56.423
That's definitely more than enough&nbsp;fertilizer for the soil for the new trees.

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:10.200
So as you can see, we didn't need the&nbsp;buckets, the neighbor were so nice

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:17.121
enough that they just dump the the whole&nbsp;cow... product 
- Manure

00:06:17.121 --> 00:06:22.686
- Manure in our... in the pickup so now it's time to unload it.

00:06:22.686 --> 00:06:27.494
- We're going to make a pile right here.

00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:48.834
Ok. So here we are in the place that Benjamin&nbsp;
took out all the roots.

00:06:48.834 --> 00:06:52.623
It's been now quite a muddy field because it has been raining&nbsp;for a couple of days.

00:06:52.623 --> 00:06:55.066
But actually we're ready for the next step.

00:06:55.066 --> 00:07:00.995
So as you see here the&nbsp;angle is quite steep, as we have it now, and the road is way more smooth

00:07:00.995 --> 00:07:05.050
so what we're going&nbsp;to do is to make this all on the same level as the road

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:08.380
so then we can start to give&nbsp;
it more shape.

00:07:09.508 --> 00:07:11.775
But then the digger didn't came

00:07:13.915 --> 00:07:17.705
But you know what came? Rain. Lots and lots of rain...

00:07:22.841 --> 00:07:24.756
Inside the office no problem

00:07:26.818 --> 00:07:28.581
but outside, yeah...

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:35.004
Water flushing down over the fields

00:07:35.004 --> 00:07:39.756
eroding the soil and creating many small streams that flush the sand and&nbsp;earth away...

00:07:41.273 --> 00:07:44.776
In a short time it created pretty deep channels as you can see here...

00:07:44.776 --> 00:07:47.881
This is the thing we hopefully will fix in this video.

00:07:53.095 --> 00:07:57.609
And the beloved waterfall was on steroids&nbsp;again. This is actually pretty cool to see.

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:06.322
And there was so much wind that our workspace&nbsp;
tarp teared apart...
- That kind of sucks

00:08:06.322 --> 00:08:09.227
Yeah, we can't really work on the land when the weather is&nbsp;like this.

00:08:09.227 --> 00:08:13.202
So we decided to take a break and solve another problem that we collected.

00:08:13.202 --> 00:08:18.335
We don't have a&nbsp;real place to hung our clothes besides this kind of nails...

00:08:18.335 --> 00:08:21.890
So now we want to make something about&nbsp;it.

00:08:21.890 --> 00:08:25.781
We have already made some projects reusing materials from waste that we generate.

00:08:25.781 --> 00:08:31.604
But this&nbsp;time we are going to go one step forward and recycle our own plastic waste in Project Kamp

00:08:31.604 --> 00:08:34.405
to transform&nbsp;it into a new product for the project...

00:08:34.405 --> 00:08:40.227
We try to avoid as much plastic as we can but sometimes&nbsp;just cannot go around.

00:08:41.978 --> 00:08:45.491
We have used products from the community a few times, like the kitchen roof,

00:08:45.491 --> 00:08:48.647
toilet or sockets. But we never made objects here.

00:08:48.647 --> 00:08:54.830
And now we are going to recycle the plastic from&nbsp;Project Kamp on the spot and running fully on solar energy.

00:08:56.970 --> 00:09:02.887
Let me first show you our waste area&nbsp;because the first step for recycling is to collect our material.

00:09:03.354 --> 00:09:06.384
Here is where all our waste&nbsp;comes every day.

00:09:06.384 --> 00:09:11.017
These buckets with regular waste get empty every week

00:09:11.017 --> 00:09:16.025
but up here we have the&nbsp;material that we will use for our project now.

00:09:19.449 --> 00:09:24.552
This is all the plastic we have been&nbsp;collecting in the last seasons...

00:09:25.447 --> 00:09:26.395
It's heavy

00:09:26.395 --> 00:09:30.852
We have been collecting mainly hard plastics,&nbsp;
especially number two and five.

00:09:30.852 --> 00:09:33.831
High density polyethylene and polypropylene.

00:09:33.831 --> 00:09:36.894
Two of the most&nbsp;used Plastics in the houses

00:09:36.894 --> 00:09:42.769
We ask the people to clean it and take out the labels before we&nbsp;put it in our bins.

00:09:42.769 --> 00:09:50.207
Let's see what we have in these buckets but let's find a dry space, so&nbsp;then I can properly show you what we have been collecting.

00:09:53.786 --> 00:09:56.064
So this is all the plastic we have.

00:09:56.064 --> 00:09:59.888
This is number two polyethylene, this is number five polypropylene...

00:09:59.888 --> 00:10:04.237
Not bad for a project of 25&nbsp;people in one season and a half.

00:10:04.237 --> 00:10:08.735
Thanks in part to the kitchen that this year they were improving&nbsp;a lot our buying system.

00:10:08.735 --> 00:10:11.631
Buying more in bulk, better suppliers.

00:10:11.631 --> 00:10:15.905
But we still have been collecting some&nbsp;of these hard plastics.

00:10:15.905 --> 00:10:23.701
It's clean, sorted, but for the next steps we need some machinery and for that we're going to use some Precious Plastic machines.

00:10:23.701 --> 00:10:30.677
Some of you probably already know what Precious
Plastic means, but sure many of you don't know what I'm talking about.

00:10:30.677 --> 00:10:33.877
So let let me explain you what&nbsp;Precious Plastic is...

00:10:33.877 --> 00:10:36.723
Dave can you help me?

00:10:38.318 --> 00:10:44.014
So Dave, I was trying to tell them what is Precious Plastic?

00:10:44.014 --> 00:10:48.185
Precious Plastic is a project that builds machines to recycle plastic.

00:10:48.185 --> 00:10:53.193
So you can have a machine where&nbsp;you throw in plastic waste and something new comes out.

00:10:53.193 --> 00:10:56.948
Ok. And why... why did it start this thing?

00:10:56.948 --> 00:11:00.791
It started because there were just a lot of plastic waste in the world

00:11:00.791 --> 00:11:03.897
and not much got recycled, less than 10%.

00:11:03.897 --> 00:11:07.983
But many people say they want to recycle plastic but they just don't have the tools.

00:11:07.983 --> 00:11:12.203
Like here you can start working with metal or wood if you have the tools.

00:11:12.203 --> 00:11:18.764
But for plastic you don't&nbsp;really have them. So try to just make the machines so people can recycle

00:11:18.764 --> 00:11:25.643
- So, do I buy the machines?&nbsp;
- No, the machines are all... well you could, but the drawings are shared open source online for free

00:11:25.643 --> 00:11:29.597
and so you can download the blueprints and build them yourselves.

00:11:29.597 --> 00:11:34.783
But if you don't want to build&nbsp;them we also have an online marketplace where you can buy them from someone else.

00:11:34.783 --> 00:11:38.609
- So,&nbsp;what if the people want to know a bit more about Precious Plastic?

00:11:38.609 --> 00:11:43.058
- I would say go to PreciousPlastic.com&nbsp;you find most of the information

00:11:43.058 --> 00:11:46.088
and there's also a whole online community with drawings you can&nbsp;download

00:11:46.088 --> 00:11:50.203
and a marketplace and a map where you can see all the workspaces around the world.

00:11:50.203 --> 00:11:54.875
- Ok, thank you Dave. So now that we know what Precious Plastic is...

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:09.666
You might know us from chopping mimosas
or building level two houses...

00:12:09.666 --> 00:12:12.923
but we also recycle plastic and teach others how to do it.

00:12:12.923 --> 00:12:17.156
When we are not in Project Kamp, we help people to set up their own recycling work spaces,

00:12:17.156 --> 00:12:19.638
we&nbsp;do events educating people about plastic,

00:12:19.638 --> 00:12:23.713
or we design recycled plastic products for other&nbsp;
precious plastic work spaces.

00:12:23.713 --> 00:12:26.003
You can find us in the Precious Plastic map.

00:12:26.003 --> 00:12:31.983
If you also want&nbsp;to recycle the plastic of your community let us know and we come in the white van&nbsp;with our machines.

00:12:31.983 --> 00:12:35.513
But we don't do it like this. We do it like this...

00:12:35.513 --> 00:12:38.941
and today we're&nbsp;going to recycle the plastic on Project Kamp.

00:12:45.912 --> 00:12:51.655
So plastic is clean, sort by type, we have&nbsp;
this machine that is called a shredder machine

00:12:51.655 --> 00:12:55.481
that what it does basically is to break down all&nbsp;
these big chunks

00:12:55.481 --> 00:12:59.541
big lids, plant pots, into small flakes.

00:13:05.377 --> 00:13:11.579
It's built in a very simple way&nbsp;and designed in order to be as much replicable as possible

00:13:11.579 --> 00:13:15.203
so everyone&nbsp;in the world can start to recycle plastic.

00:13:21.467 --> 00:13:26.735
So sometimes we need to cut the&nbsp;pieces because they are quite big and the hopper of the machine is a small area.

00:13:26.735 --> 00:13:31.058
So we cut them up in small pieces and then they're shredded.

00:14:01.402 --> 00:14:04.240
One kilo of polypropylene we got from our waste

00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:11.211
as&nbsp;you could see first before it's shredding, it takes a lot of space to store it, but now...

00:14:11.211 --> 00:14:15.595
as soon&nbsp;as we have it shredded, almost doesn't take any space.

00:14:18.280 --> 00:14:21.438
So now that we already shredded&nbsp;all the PP, we're going for HDPE.

00:14:21.438 --> 00:14:25.806
And as you see we also have some plastics that&nbsp;we cannot avoid here.

00:14:25.806 --> 00:14:29.915
This for example is the jerrycan where we normally buy our&nbsp;olive oil in

00:14:29.915 --> 00:14:37.412
but we also have bottles like contact lens liquid or bottle caps from&nbsp;other bottles we have.

00:14:37.412 --> 00:14:39.701
So let's back to recycling.

00:14:44.530 --> 00:14:46.050
Very satisfying...

00:14:52.737 --> 00:14:54.173
- Funny guys, huh?

00:15:18.280 --> 00:15:22.414
HDPE is shredded, let's see how much did we get...

00:15:25.760 --> 00:15:28.173
871 grams

00:15:41.320 --> 00:15:46.991
So now it's time to write down which kind of&nbsp;plastic is in each box

00:15:47.730 --> 00:15:53.026
to not lose the track, so then we know which plastic we are using&nbsp;when injecting

00:15:54.894 --> 00:15:57.237
So now we have all our shredded plastic

00:15:57.237 --> 00:16:03.483
we finished with this machine, so it's&nbsp;time to swap machines and start to actually melt plastic.

00:16:12.275 --> 00:16:15.131
Ok let's open this container up.

00:16:18.631 --> 00:16:23.575
For the next step in the&nbsp;recycling process we're going to use the injection machine which I&nbsp;have right here.

00:16:23.575 --> 00:16:28.520
It's another machine out of the Precious Plastic universe. How we do that? I show you right now.

00:16:31.240 --> 00:16:33.382
So for this machine we are using molds

00:16:33.382 --> 00:16:36.787
and&nbsp;for each product that you want to have you need to have a different mold.

00:16:36.787 --> 00:16:40.737
This time&nbsp;we designed a product that served for many places on the land.

00:16:40.737 --> 00:16:43.992
So the first product&nbsp;we're going to make out of polypropolene.

00:16:43.992 --> 00:16:48.962
So now we pour the plastic we just shredded into the hopper of the injection machine.

00:16:56.495 --> 00:17:01.638
Ok. So that means that the&nbsp;plastic has been heating up for 10 minutes...

00:17:01.638 --> 00:17:04.262
and we're going to now inject the first product

00:17:05.701 --> 00:17:10.848
we take off the lid and we connect the molds to the machine

00:17:13.455 --> 00:17:15.396
and now: time to inject.

00:17:15.863 --> 00:17:17.476
So, pressure

00:17:22.300 --> 00:17:26.377
Luckily the plastic cools out very&nbsp;fast, so we can already take off the mold

00:17:39.320 --> 00:17:40.464
and the product...

00:17:55.365 --> 00:17:59.840
So this is the mold how it looks&nbsp;from the inside, and we can make four hooks

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:04.400
at the same time and this mold also has the&nbsp;
possibility of changing the type of plastic.

00:18:04.400 --> 00:18:10.580
Here we have a little piece that says PP, which&nbsp;
we will insert in the mold in the middle,

00:18:10.580 --> 00:18:12.413
but we can also place the HDPE.

00:18:12.841 --> 00:18:17.450
Ok, we have injected&nbsp;some hooks and we're going to bring them now to the workspace container

00:18:17.450 --> 00:18:19.868
where Adrian is going to tell you a bit more about the hooks.

00:18:25.042 --> 00:18:25.637
Come!

00:18:26.182 --> 00:18:28.342
Ok, so here we are...

00:18:28.342 --> 00:18:30.109
We still&nbsp;need to post-process our hook.

00:18:30.109 --> 00:18:35.864
As you may notice we don't have the holes to go through&nbsp;to, in order to screw it into the wall.

00:18:35.864 --> 00:18:44.321
So for this mission we have this little tool where&nbsp;
the hook fits exactly,

00:18:44.321 --> 00:18:54.852
so we drill through, we make countersink and then we will have&nbsp;a screw that stays flush with our surface

00:18:56.875 --> 00:19:04.442
So the idea from the beginning of the project&nbsp;was to make a product that we could afterwards open source and the people can replicate

00:19:04.442 --> 00:19:07.380
and&nbsp;that serves as much situations as possible...

00:19:07.380 --> 00:19:11.816
And we've been working in this mold&nbsp;
with Fritz from Easymoulds

00:19:11.816 --> 00:19:14.790
So you can find how to in the Precious Plastic community&nbsp;platform.

00:19:14.790 --> 00:19:18.087
Where you can find the drawings of these hooks

00:19:18.087 --> 00:19:25.945
of this mold for the people to&nbsp;start to recycle plastic into this fantastic hooks.

00:20:00.320 --> 00:20:05.160
So hooks are ready to be installed and&nbsp;I already know a couple of places that

00:20:05.160 --> 00:20:07.844
could use one of these hooks...

00:20:07.844 --> 00:20:09.824
Let me show&nbsp;you.

00:20:09.824 --> 00:20:15.925
So, here as you can see is one of the central hanging clothes in the whole&nbsp;Project Kamp.

00:20:15.925 --> 00:20:25.592
We have put some nails. Was a bit of a temporary solution but that's&nbsp;a bit painful always for the clothes so...

00:20:46.000 --> 00:20:49.280
I'm in the shower. This is another painful place

00:20:49.280 --> 00:20:53.880
for our towels always scared to&nbsp;break my towel when I come here, so...

00:21:02.720 --> 00:21:06.890
ready, nice, soft and waterproof.

00:21:28.468 --> 00:21:30.526
Much better.

00:21:31.333 --> 00:21:35.270
Now if you want to get&nbsp;inspired for other people around the world recycling plastic

00:21:35.270 --> 00:21:38.505
make sure to follow the YouTube&nbsp;
channel of Precious Plastic.

00:21:38.505 --> 00:21:41.528
If you want to make your own hook

00:21:41.528 --> 00:21:46.152
remember that it's an open source design so&nbsp;
you can find all the information in how to

00:21:46.152 --> 00:21:51.251
in the Precious Plastic website you can find the link below.&nbsp;If not you can always find a Precious Plastic

00:21:51.251 --> 00:21:56.960
workspace around your area and you can collaborate&nbsp;
with them. You just need to go in the map and find them.

00:21:59.839 --> 00:22:04.648
Finally after all the rain, the sun came back,&nbsp;
so we could continue planting the trees together.

00:22:04.648 --> 00:22:09.800
However now we actually don't really have the time&nbsp;
anymore because it's the season ending and people

00:22:09.800 --> 00:22:15.880
are heading home. So we wanted to use this precious&nbsp;
sunny day so everyone can pack and dry their stuff.

00:22:15.880 --> 00:22:21.729
It was actually really nice being able to do this on a dry day. Packing a wet tent is just the worst

00:22:26.514 --> 00:22:30.595
and all together we also tied it up and&nbsp;
clean the whole Kamp.

00:22:38.571 --> 00:22:43.758
And it was the last day&nbsp;of the season, so that night the people organized a fancy dinner to celebrate.

00:22:43.758 --> 00:22:48.360
It was really nice. Here's a sneak peek on what it was like...

00:22:56.725 --> 00:23:02.551
 also most people ever&nbsp;at once in the sketchy ruin and still didn't&nbsp;break down maybe it's not so sketchy after all

00:23:11.538 --> 00:23:16.940
- What's happening today Adrian? 
- Everyone's&nbsp;leaving, not everyone, many people

00:23:16.940 --> 00:23:20.177
- Who's not leaving?
- I'm not...

00:23:47.171 --> 00:23:50.011
This is not what we said. You would cry, you said...

00:23:50.400 --> 00:23:52.829
Almost... thank you guys!

00:23:53.490 --> 00:23:58.430
- Waving out&nbsp;
- I can, I can do the...

00:23:58.430 --> 00:24:00.224
- This looks so staged!

00:24:10.666 --> 00:24:14.760
- Final words

00:24:14.760 --> 00:24:20.000
- Was nice 
- Have a nice journey! Bye!

00:24:31.511 --> 00:24:33.607
At this point there are still&nbsp;a few people in the Kamp

00:24:33.607 --> 00:24:37.711
and it was a sunny day so finally&nbsp;the big digger came for the next step.

00:24:41.290 --> 00:24:45.132
We're going to reshape the slope&nbsp;so the difference in height is less steep.

00:24:47.466 --> 00:24:52.870
At this point everything is still quite wet&nbsp;and muddy, but doesn't seem to be a problem for him.

00:25:33.200 --> 00:25:36.640
So we finished up smoothing this&nbsp;area and now we're going to take on

00:25:36.640 --> 00:25:39.680
the step of digging the cable and&nbsp;we are making use of Bruno being

00:25:39.680 --> 00:25:43.276
here with his big machine, digging&nbsp;
a cable from the office to Base Kamp.

00:25:57.827 --> 00:26:00.816
As you see this is the trench. 80 cm deep.

00:26:00.816 --> 00:26:04.928
And we're going to have the internet, the&nbsp;water and the electricity in here.

00:26:04.928 --> 00:26:07.598
So the cables will be placed later on

00:26:07.598 --> 00:26:10.059
- Bit&nbsp;of brown.
- Treasures?

00:26:10.059 --> 00:26:13.127
- No treasures yet, not yet.

00:26:55.000 --> 00:26:57.600
- What do you think Julie?
- Got a rainbow...

00:26:59.640 --> 00:27:00.700
I don't know what I think.

00:27:00.700 --> 00:27:05.464
So we have dug the trench from Base Kamp to the office...

00:27:05.464 --> 00:27:07.772
so we can dig all the pipes now

00:27:07.772 --> 00:27:14.338
and then in the future we don't&nbsp;need to open the soil again. So we can let the grass and the trees grow in the top layer.

00:27:15.038 --> 00:27:18.277
But&nbsp;let me show you first what we're going to lay here...

00:27:21.895 --> 00:27:27.551
A connection of water, electricity and&nbsp;internet. Electricity and internet go inside this pipe.

00:27:27.551 --> 00:27:31.309
With the internet still not clear how&nbsp;we are going to connect everything

00:27:31.309 --> 00:27:34.366
but since we are already laying the electricity inside&nbsp;
the pipe

00:27:34.366 --> 00:27:40.459
we are going to connect already the ethernet cable for the future, to see how&nbsp;we connect everything

00:27:40.459 --> 00:27:47.254
When it comes out to infrastructure we have a lot of pipes that are&nbsp;mainly made out of plastic...

00:27:47.254 --> 00:27:52.974
everything is wrapped in plastic, cables protection, so...

00:27:52.974 --> 00:27:58.198
We are kind of new, so we don't know many other alternative. Let us know if you know something else...

00:27:58.198 --> 00:28:03.565
But for&nbsp;now we're going to install the standard in the area.

00:28:26.471 --> 00:28:28.840
Delicate part coming...

00:28:28.840 --> 00:28:32.464
Pipe is laid. Inside the pipe there's this thread

00:28:32.464 --> 00:28:34.952
to pull two cables through the pipe

00:28:34.952 --> 00:28:39.334
is&nbsp;our longest distance that we pull a cable through.

00:28:40.190 --> 00:28:45.589
So if we lose this thread&nbsp;it's going to be very painful.

00:28:45.589 --> 00:28:47.630
Pull smooth, pull.

00:28:55.645 --> 00:28:57.520
How far do you think we are?

00:28:57.870 --> 00:29:03.640
So, meanwhile&nbsp;Adrian is sorting that out I'm going to start digging.

00:29:38.485 --> 00:29:42.169
Ok. So right now I'm standing on top of the&nbsp;
cable so that one is covered.

00:29:42.169 --> 00:29:46.221
And that brings&nbsp;us to the next point which is this line...

00:29:47.271 --> 00:29:49.823
You don't see a line...

00:29:49.823 --> 00:29:50.888
This way.

00:29:50.888 --> 00:29:53.664
This line intentionally was going to be a swale

00:29:53.664 --> 00:30:00.797
But due&nbsp;to the lack of manpower and timing of the wetness we are going to make it a ditch.

00:30:00.797 --> 00:30:03.315
It&nbsp;might not look as a proper swale in the end

00:30:03.315 --> 00:30:09.240
but we're going to give it a try with little&nbsp;manpower and a quite difficult soil to work on...

00:30:33.280 --> 00:30:36.477
- This will even look to 
- Do you think we're&nbsp;going to make it?

00:30:38.228 --> 00:30:41.015
Yeah! Just a couple of meters more...

00:30:51.987 --> 00:30:56.383
We tried, yeah. We really did, but it was&nbsp;already late and it started to rain again.

00:30:56.383 --> 00:30:58.493
Then it got dark.

00:30:58.493 --> 00:31:00.318
It was not realistic to&nbsp;continue.

00:31:00.318 --> 00:31:04.238
It's been very rainy this week but we realized the forecast for tomorrow is sunny

00:31:04.238 --> 00:31:05.613
and we're in a rush...

00:31:05.613 --> 00:31:08.217
So we invited another digger

00:31:46.480 --> 00:31:48.901
Swale one, done

00:31:50.000 --> 00:31:51.917
now we're going to the next...

00:32:13.120 --> 00:32:17.960
One, two, three swales

00:32:43.320 --> 00:32:45.841
Cool! Let me tell you something about the&nbsp;trees we actually bought.

00:32:45.841 --> 00:32:51.051
We chose to have a good variation based on the consumption that we&nbsp;have during the season.

00:32:51.051 --> 00:32:56.489
We also make sure that the trees actually give fruits during the season&nbsp;when we are with more people.

00:32:56.489 --> 00:32:57.040
Let me grab them.

00:33:00.880 --> 00:33:02.981
Ok. So this is our big tree family...

00:33:02.981 --> 00:33:06.680
The one that&nbsp;we are going to need to take care of for the next years

00:33:06.680 --> 00:33:11.511
and we are all going to take care of them&nbsp;a bit differently. For example here the citrus trees

00:33:11.511 --> 00:33:14.044
we are going to plant them a bit higher&nbsp;up on the field.

00:33:14.044 --> 00:33:19.946
They are closer to Base Kamp. This means that they get less frost because it's&nbsp;less cold up here than down in the field.

00:33:19.946 --> 00:33:24.316
Further we have here a cherry tree which we want to give&nbsp;a bit more space

00:33:24.316 --> 00:33:25.716
so it can grow bigger.

00:33:25.716 --> 00:33:28.827
But then we have for example here the pears and also the&nbsp;apples

00:33:28.827 --> 00:33:34.210
and they don't need that much space they are a bit smaller. So we decided to blend them&nbsp;on the swales, on top of the swales.

00:33:34.210 --> 00:33:36.557
Ok. So as you see the swales are done.

00:33:36.557 --> 00:33:39.907
Nice and smooth. Really nice that the digger came to give us a hand.

00:33:39.907 --> 00:33:44.897
And actually I will explain you later&nbsp;how this works because we're going to plant some trees

00:33:47.426 --> 00:33:49.229
but not alone, with some friends.

00:33:50.435 --> 00:33:53.610
Ok. So as you can see we found quite some friends

00:33:53.610 --> 00:33:55.681
luckily, because all the other&nbsp;people already left

00:33:55.681 --> 00:33:57.770
and these people are coming from the area.

00:33:57.770 --> 00:34:00.221
To make the job easier&nbsp;we divided them in teams.

00:34:00.221 --> 00:34:01.718
So we have a digger team

00:34:02.691 --> 00:34:05.053
but we also have a manure team

00:34:05.364 --> 00:34:08.188
and&nbsp;then Adrian and I will be distributing all the trees...

00:34:10.445 --> 00:34:12.402
and the dogs will run around.

00:34:12.791 --> 00:34:16.215
Ok. So to not lose any time we're going to start.

00:34:20.768 --> 00:34:26.514
This kind of hard pack down&nbsp;below. It seems like it would be easy in the mud...

00:34:28.926 --> 00:34:34.306
We're digging some holes, like one meter diameter and&nbsp;like 40 cm deep...

00:34:34.306 --> 00:34:37.760
and then we'll bring some manure in...

00:35:10.800 --> 00:35:13.589
Step number two: put some manure.

00:35:40.626 --> 00:35:43.767
- Should be pointed to north, this&nbsp;one... 
- This one

00:35:43.767 --> 00:35:49.811
- And you don't need to cut that, cuz this is the womb of the plant and you&nbsp;don't want to expose it to the sun

00:35:49.811 --> 00:35:52.606
- With the womb pointing to the north

00:35:52.956 --> 00:35:54.937
- Learning by doing

00:35:57.077 --> 00:36:04.668
So here we have&nbsp;plum tree or in Portuguese abrunheiro. Did I say good abrunheiro?

00:36:17.445 --> 00:36:25.260
I've planted the pessegueiro. They&nbsp;give lovely plums or peaches, I don't know, but they give something...

00:36:25.260 --> 00:36:30.032
We have a golden apple tree, golden delicious I think we would call it...

00:36:30.032 --> 00:36:32.890
A cherry tree, cerejeira...

00:36:32.890 --> 00:36:35.933
Peach tree that I just planted...

00:36:37.139 --> 00:36:41.224
We are using some wood chips to&nbsp;cover the soil in the base of the trees...

00:36:42.080 --> 00:36:43.919
Let's go!

00:37:05.157 --> 00:37:10.233
And finally we are seeding some cover&nbsp;crops to help holding the soil in place.

00:37:11.246 --> 00:37:14.893
I'm just playing with a dog I'm not&nbsp;doing anything useful.

00:37:14.893 --> 00:37:18.143
You will get a lot of clever in your belly!

00:37:19.038 --> 00:37:23.563
- But Rita, are you&nbsp;feeding the soil or feeding the dog?
- Not sure!

00:37:27.843 --> 00:37:33.620
All right finally the job is done. All the&nbsp;trees are planted, the landscape has changed a lot

00:37:33.620 --> 00:37:37.520
and now we just need to wait for nature&nbsp;to do its thing during the winter.

00:37:37.520 --> 00:37:43.014
But actually this landscape is made in a certain way. Let me&nbsp;explain you from the top:

00:37:43.014 --> 00:37:46.732
we made this swale on top to collect the water and another one below

00:37:46.732 --> 00:37:50.330
both are almost horizontal so the water doesn't flow out fast

00:37:50.330 --> 00:37:53.386
then we made the third one which&nbsp;is more inclined

00:37:53.386 --> 00:37:58.871
and here we added a ditch to connect them and made sure the water on the&nbsp;road can flow in as well.

00:37:58.871 --> 00:38:01.887
So the water should flow something like this...

00:38:01.887 --> 00:38:05.480
And in the bottom one&nbsp;it should flow faster because of the angle.

00:38:05.480 --> 00:38:09.130
This is done because we don't want the ground to get&nbsp;
too wet close to the ruin.

00:38:09.130 --> 00:38:11.747
Because it's half in the ground and will leak inside.

00:38:11.747 --> 00:38:15.645
But we can&nbsp;always add obstacles to slow down the water flow if we want...

00:38:15.645 --> 00:38:19.310
Finally the water will flow out&nbsp;there into the big field.

00:38:19.310 --> 00:38:26.003
And most of the trees are planted on the bottom of the slope so they&nbsp;can benefit from the water that soaks into the ground.

00:38:26.003 --> 00:38:33.079
All of this should give the rain water&nbsp;more time to absorb into the ground and keep it more moist for the fruit trees throughout the&nbsp;year.

00:38:33.079 --> 00:38:36.329
Now we just have to wait for the sun and the rain to do their magic.

00:38:36.329 --> 00:38:39.981
Quite exciting to see&nbsp;how this will behave when the winter rain comes in.

00:38:39.981 --> 00:38:44.868
It's pretty cool to imagine a big amount&nbsp;of fruit trees here since we have Base Kamp here,

00:38:44.868 --> 00:38:47.935
Community Center here, and future living&nbsp;area here

00:38:47.935 --> 00:38:49.768
where the big trailer is built.

00:38:49.768 --> 00:38:52.104
So, many people could benefit from it.

00:38:52.104 --> 00:38:56.533
Ok. Enough&nbsp;about the trees. Enough about the season of Project Kamp

